ted.</li><li>Those most affected are not involved from the outset.</li></ul><h2>How to put it right</h2><p>The first step is always to make clear what should change and how that change will be visible. Without it, no programme can be judged a success, and the discussion stops at taste.</p><h2>What changes once it is applied</h2><p>The fastest-felt change is usually not in the headline numbers but in less rework and fewer repeated arguments. The numbers follow later, once the new habit has held for a few months.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>No single approach suits every organisation. But starting from the right question is almost always cheaper than fixing a programme already running in the wrong direction.</p>\"}','agenda/yqrimxzhzqe36nkdloqt0ayibmnz7bqxh0hytupf.webp',NULL,'Jakarta Selatan, DKI Jakarta','published',0,749,'2026-05-15 02:00:00',NULL,NULL,'2026-09-14 08:15:07','2026-09-14 08:15:07'),(19,1,NULL,'{\"id\":\"Asesmen Kompetensi Sebagai Dasar Promosi\",\"en\":\"Competency Assessment as a Basis for Promotion\"}','asesmen-kompetensi-sebagai-dasar-promosi','{\"id\":\"Keputusan promosi yang hanya berdasarkan penilaian atasan langsung sulit dipertahankan ketika dipertanyakan.\",\"en\":\"Promotion decisions resting only on line-manager judgement are hard to defend when questioned.\"}','{\"id\":\"<p>Keputusan promosi yang hanya berdasarkan penilaian atasan langsung sulit dipertahankan ketika dipertanyakan.</p><h2>Masalah yang sering terjadi</h2><p>Dalam pendampingan kami di berbagai perusahaan, persoalan ini hampir selalu muncul dalam bentuk yang sama: keputusan diambil lebih dulu, alasannya disusun belakangan. Akibatnya sumber daya terpakai untuk memperbaiki gejala, sementara penyebabnya tetap di tempat.</p><h3>Yang perlu diperhatikan</h3><ul><li>Ukuran keberhasilan tidak disepakati sebelum program dimulai.</li><li>Data yang sudah dimiliki perusahaan jarang dilihat kembali.</li><li>Pihak yang paling terdampak tidak dilibatkan sejak awal.</li></ul><h2>Cara memperbaikinya</h2><p>Langkah pertama selalu memperjelas apa yang hendak berubah dan bagaimana perubahan itu akan terlihat. Tanpa itu, program apa pun sulit dinilai berhasil atau tidak, dan pembahasannya berhenti pada selera.</p><h2>Apa yang berubah setelah diterapkan</h2><p>Perubahan yang paling cepat terasa biasanya bukan pada angka besar, melainkan pada berkurangnya pekerjaan ulang dan perdebatan berulang. Angka menyusul kemudian, setelah kebiasaan barunya bertahan beberapa bulan.</p><h2>Kesimpulan</h2><p>Tidak ada pendekatan tunggal yang cocok untuk semua organisasi. Namun memulai dari pertanyaan yang tepat hampir selalu lebih murah daripada memperbaiki program yang sudah terlanjur berjalan ke arah yang salah.</p>\",\"en\":\"<p>Promotion decisions resting only on line-manager judgement are hard to defend when questioned.</p><h2>The problem we keep seeing</h2><p>Across the companies we work with, this issue shows up in almost the same shape every time: the decision comes first and the reasoning is assembled afterwards. Resources then go into treating symptoms while the cause stays put.</p><h3>What to watch for</h3><ul><li>Success measures are not agreed before the programme starts.</li><li>Data the company already holds is rarely revisited.</li><li>Those most affected are not involved from the outset.</li></ul><h2>How to put it right</h2><p>The first step is always to make clear what should change and how that change will be visible. Without it, no programme can be judged a success, and the discussion stops at taste.</p><h2>What changes once it is applied</h2><p>The fastest-felt change is usually not in the headline numbers but in less rework and fewer repeated arguments. The numbers follow later, once the new habit has held for a few months.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>No single approach suits every organisation. But starting from the right question is almost always cheaper than fixing a programme already running in the wrong direction.</p>\"}','services/femt61x5fku1xeo71dcnez3elsbke6pe4hzgltpm.webp',NULL,'Serang, Banten','published',0,786,'2026-05-08 02:00:00',NULL,NULL,'2026-09-14 08:15:07','2026-09-14 08:15:07'),(20,5,NULL,'{\"id\":\"Menyiapkan Karyawan Menghadapi Perubahan Sistem\",\"en\":\"Preparing Employees for a System Change\"}','menyiapkan-karyawan-menghadapi-perubahan-sistem','{\"id\":\"Sistem baru gagal dipakai bukan karena rumit, melainkan karena kekhawatiran penggunanya tidak pernah dijawab.\",\"en\":\"New systems go unused not because they are complex but because users\' worries were never addressed.\"}','{\"id\":\"<p>Sistem baru gagal dipakai bukan karena rumit, melainkan karena kekhawatiran penggunanya tidak pernah dijawab.</p><h2>Masalah yang sering terjadi</h2><p>Dalam pendampingan kami di berbagai perusahaan, persoalan ini hampir selalu muncul dalam bentuk yang sama: keputusan diambil lebih dulu, alasannya disusun belakangan. Akibatnya sumber daya terpakai untuk memperbaiki gejala, sementara penyebabnya tetap di tempat.</p><h3>Yang perlu diperhatikan</h3><ul><li>Ukuran keberhasilan tidak disepakati sebelum program dimulai.</li><li>Data yang sudah dimiliki perusahaan jarang dilihat kembali.</li><li>Pihak yang paling terdampak tidak dilibatkan sejak awal.</li></ul><h2>Cara memperbaikinya</h2><p>Langkah pertama selalu memperjelas apa yang hendak berubah dan bagaimana perubahan itu akan terlihat. Tanpa itu, program apa pun sulit dinilai berhasil atau tidak, dan pembahasannya berhenti pada selera.</p><h2>Apa yang berubah setelah diterapkan</h2><p>Perubahan yang paling cepat terasa biasanya bukan pada angka besar, melainkan pada berkurangnya pekerjaan ulang dan perdebatan berulang. Angka menyusul kemudian, setelah kebiasaan barunya bertahan beberapa bulan.</p><h2>Kesimpulan</h2><p>Tidak ada pendekatan tunggal yang cocok untuk semua organisasi. Namun memulai dari pertanyaan yang tepat hampir selalu lebih murah daripada memperbaiki program yang sudah terlanjur berjalan ke arah yang salah.</p>\",\"en\":\"<p>New systems go unused not because they are complex but because users\' worries were never addressed.</p><h2>The problem we keep seeing</h2><p>Across the companies we work with, this issue shows up in almost the same shape every time: the decision comes first and the reasoning is assembled afterwards. Resources then go into treating symptoms while the cause stays put.</p><h3>What to watch for</h3><ul><li>Success measures are not agreed before the programme starts.</li><li>Data the company already holds is rarely revisited.</li><li>Those most affected are not involved from the outset.</li></ul><h2>How to put it right</h2><p>The first step is always to make clear what should change and how that change will be visible. Without it, no programme can be judged a success, and the discussion stops at taste.</p><h2>What changes once it is applied</h2><p>The fastest-felt change is usually not in the headline numbers but in less rework and fewer repeated arguments. The numbers follow later, once the new habit has held for a few months.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>No single approach suits every organisation. But starting from the right question is almost always cheaper than fixing a programme already running in the wrong direction.</p>\"}','services/gzefafk6sptj1kqryp3d9evmmvpd1raeseiqyfkv.webp',NULL,'Jakarta Barat, DKI Jakarta','published',0,823,'2026-05-01 02:00:00',NULL,NULL,'2026-09-14 08:15:07','2026-09-14 08:15:07');
